<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Duties (system 9: AI). A duty is a standing goal a pawn carries while it is on somebody else's map, and
    its thinkNode is the whole of it — see SimWorld.AI.DutyDef for what this replaces (RimWorld's
    Lord/LordToil/Trigger graph) and exactly what that translation costs.

    The tiers below are read in order and are the phases of an assault, which in RimWorld are separate
    LordToils with Triggers between them. Here the tree is re-evaluated every time the pawn needs a job, so
    each tier's own "do I apply?" is asked continuously and the phase change falls out of the ordering:
    while there is an enemy anywhere on this map, close on it; when there is not — everyone down, or the
    town sealed off behind something the squad cannot path around — leave.

    The fight tier is NOT in here. It sits above this whole node, in ThinkTrees_Humanlike.xml, because
    fighting back is not something a pawn needs a duty to do: a raider and a citizen at arm's length both
    swing, and that tier was already there before duties existed (SimWorld.AI.CombatPostureUtility).

    WHAT IS MISSING BETWEEN THE TWO, and why it is absent rather than stubbed. RimWorld's AssaultColony duty
    has a JobGiver_AITrashBuildingsDistant here: with no colonist left standing, raiders smash the colony
    itself. This port cannot ask that question at all — Verse.Thing has a faction and SimWorld.Things.Thing
    does not, so no building on any map belongs to anybody and "the enemy's structures" has no candidate set
    to draw from. Wiring a tier to a set that is empty by construction would look like behaviour and be
    none, so a raid here goes straight from "nobody left to fight" to "go home". Giving Things a faction is
    the prerequisite, and it is a Things-module change with consequences well beyond raids (ownership,
    stealing, claiming, turret allegiance), not something to smuggle in through a duty.
  -->

  <DutyDef>
    <defName>AssaultSettlement</defName>
    <thinkNode Class="SimWorld.AI.ThinkNode_Priority">
      <subNodes>
        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_AIGotoNearestHostile" />
        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_ExitMap" />
      </subNodes>
    </thinkNode>
  </DutyDef>

</Defs>
